From 3a81c141779577b81c40b8679eee616b99705ee2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: voxlo-dev <leofemu@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 09:01:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan: decode PTQ1_0 through a byte-to-trits table and word
 loads

The PTQ1_0 shaders decoded every weight element on its own: one byte load, a
three-way range branch and a loop of up to four dependent multiply-mask steps
(v = (v*3) & 0xFF) to reach the wanted base-3 digit. Per 128-element block that
is 128 byte loads and ~320 serial ALU ops, which is why the kernel ran ALU-bound
at 1.6 tok/s on an RX 570 while Q4_K_M on the same card is bandwidth-bound (#185).

Replace it with a 256-entry table, byte -> five trits, generated from the CPU
codec in ggml-quants.c (q = (uint8_t)(b * 3^n); trit = (q * 3) >> 8) and stored
as 2-bit two's-complement fields so a single bitfieldExtract yields the signed
value. The table lives in shared memory and is filled by init_iq_shmem, the same
mechanism the IQ grids use. The block is read through a new
block_ptq1_0_packed32 (seven 32-bit words) so four or eight consecutive elements
cost one or two word loads. The element -> byte mapping now lives in one place,
ptq1_0_locate in ptq1_0.glsl, used by mul_mat_vec (dequantize/dequantize4),
mul_mm (eight elements per group) and dequant_ptq1_0.comp.

Bit-exact by construction: test-backend-ops -b Vulkan0 -p ptq1_0 passes for
MUL_MAT, MUL_MAT_ID, GET_ROWS and CPY against the CPU backend, as before.

Measured on Ternary-Bonsai-2-27B-PTQ1_0 (RX 570, RADV, Mesa 26.1.2,
RADV_PERFTEST=nogttspill, clocks auto, q8_0/q4_0 KV, -fa on):

                     before      after
  generation, 16k    633 ms/tok  143 ms/tok   (1.58 -> 7.0 tok/s)
  generation, 48k    633 ms/tok  143 ms/tok
  prompt, 847 tok    36 tok/s    54 tok/s

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
